        },
        {
          "type": "expression",
          "answer": "5 * s + 10",
          "marks": 1,
          "prompt": "We want to solve the equation \\(3(2s - 1) = 5(s + 2)\\). Expanding the left-hand side gives \\(6s - 3\\) (there are \\(3 \\times 2\\) lots of \\(s\\) and \\(3 \\times (-1) = -3\\) others), and expanding the right-hand side gives ___. The solution to the equation is \\(s = \\) ___.",
          "sampleRange": [
            0,
            1
          ],
          "symbols": [
            "s"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "expression",
          "answer": "13",
          "marks": 1,
          "prompt": "",
          "sampleRange": [
            0,
            1
          ],
          "symbols": []
        },
        {
          "type": "expression",
          "answer": "10 * t + 50",
          "marks": 1,
          "prompt": "A cheetah can run at 31 metres per second, so can cover a distance of \\(31t\\) metres after \\(t\\) seconds. A world-class human sprinter can run at around \\(10\\) metres per second. If we give the human a \\(50\\) metre head start, then after \\(t\\) seconds they can cover a distance of ___ metres (including the head-start). By writing and solving an equation, find the number of seconds it would take a cheetah to catch up to the human (you can answer to one decimal place): ___.",
          "sampleRange": [
            0,
            1
          ],
          "symbols": [
            "t"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "number",
          "answer": "50 / 21",
          "marks": 1,
          "prompt": "",
          "tolerance": {
            "kind": "absolute",
            "value": 0.05
          },
          "unit": "none"
        }
      ],
      "picture": {
        "kind": "curves",
        "items": [
          {
            "expr": "5 * x - 4",
            "label": "5x − 4",
            "from": "0",
            "to": "5"
          },
          {
            "expr": "2 * x + 5",
            "label": "2x + 5",
            "from": "0",
            "to": "5"
          }
        ]
      },
      "statement": "When an equation involves multiple copies of a variable, also known as like-terms, the first step is usually to combine them. For example, to solve the equation\n$$ 5x-4=2x+5, $$\nwe can first subtract \\(2x\\) from both sides to give\n$$ 5x-2x-4=5. $$\nHaving \\(5\\) lots of \\(x\\) and removing \\(2\\) lots of \\(x\\) leaves us with \\(3\\) lots of \\(x\\), so the left-hand side of the equation is \\(3x-4\\). We can then add \\(4\\) to both sides to get \\(3x=9\\), so the solution is \\(x=3\\).",
      "tags": [
        "algebra"
      ],
      "variables": []
    },
